---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.6 (Day 1) — Dungeon Crawler RPG Build: Combat &amp; Duel Engine</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.1, 2.9 · 90-Minute Design-First RPG Build (Day 1 of 2).</strong> Over the
    next two classes you will build <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>—an authentic RPG engine combining
    indeterminate <code>while</code> loops (fighting until one combatant falls) with fixed
    <code>for</code> loops (walking floors and rooms). Today you design and build the combat layer
    (Parts 1 and 2); tomorrow in <code>4.6b</code> you wrap your duel engine inside multi-room
    floors and a multi-floor campaign.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (Download Both Starter Files):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonLoop.java" download>DungeonLoop.java</a> — starter file for
            both days with Javadocs, stubs, and a <strong>Dungeon Campaign Sandbox</strong> in
            <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a> — your dice engine from Unit 1.7.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions for loop
            conditions (De Morgan's Law!), dead-monster guards, overkill clamping, and crit/fumble
            branch priority.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; fight in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste it into <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and test your
            combat engine with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java DungeonLoop.java
java DungeonLoop</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (Day 1 Verification):</strong> Unlock <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code>
        at the bottom of this page and aim to pass all <strong>6 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> on
        your very first run (Parts 3–4 are tomorrow!).
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Deterministic Duel Loop</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>simulateDuelToDeath(int playerHP, int playerDmgPerHit, int monsterHP, int monsterDmgPerHit)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Before plugging in random dice rolls, every good game engine builds a
            <strong>deterministic duel simulator</strong> that tomorrow's multi-room floor crawler
            will call over and over. Given fixed attack values, it resolves a single one-on-one
            fight to the finish and tells the caller how much HP the hero came out with.
        </p>
        <h4>Turn Rules, Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>int playerHP</code>, <code>int monsterHP</code> — starting HP of each fighter.</li>
            <li><code>int playerDmgPerHit</code>, <code>int monsterDmgPerHit</code> — flat damage
                dealt per strike.</li>
            <li><strong>Combat Sequence:</strong> While both fighters are alive
                (<code>HP &gt; 0</code>): (1) player strikes first, subtracting
                <code>playerDmgPerHit</code> from <code>monsterHP</code>; (2) <em>only if the
                monster is still alive</em> (<code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>), the monster strikes
                back, subtracting <code>monsterDmgPerHit</code> from <code>playerHP</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> the player's remaining HP after the
                duel, <strong>clamped at <code>0</code></strong> if <code>playerHP</code> dropped
                negative.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(20, 6, 12, 5)</code> &rarr; R1: m <code>6</code>, p <code>15</code>; R2: m
                <code>0</code> (dies before swinging!) &rarr; returns <code>15</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(10, 15, 12, 99)</code> &rarr; R1: m <code>-3</code> (one-shot kill; monster
                never swings!) &rarr; returns <code>10</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(10, 3, 20, 6)</code> &rarr; R1: m <code>17</code>, p <code>4</code>; R2: m
                <code>14</code>, p <code>-2</code> &rarr; clamped to <code>0</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(18, 5, 0, 10)</code> &rarr; monster starts dead, loop executes <code>0</code>
                times &rarr; returns <code>18</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code></h3>
        <p>Connect Unit 3's De Morgan's Laws to Unit 4's combat loop design:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; De Morgan Condition:</strong> Why must this use a
                <code>while</code> loop rather than a <code>for</code> loop? The fight <em>stops</em>
                when <code>playerHP &lt;= 0 || monsterHP &lt;= 0</code>. Use De Morgan's Law to
                explain why the <code>while</code> continuation condition must use
                <code>&amp;&amp;</code> instead of <code>||</code> (what happens if you mistakenly
                write <code>playerHP &gt; 0 || monsterHP &gt; 0</code>?).</li>
            <li><strong>Intra-Round Order &amp; Clamping Edge Cases:</strong> What two bugs happen
                if you forget (a) the <code>if (monsterHP &gt; 0)</code> check before the monster's
                counterattack, and (b) clamping <code>playerHP</code> to <code>0</code> after the
                loop exits?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and check the Part 1 duel outputs in
                <code>java DungeonLoop</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Live Dice Combat</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>runCombat(...)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Now upgrade the deterministic duel loop into a live 5e dice encounter using
            <code>Dice.roll(int sides)</code> from Unit 1.7. Instead of returning exact remaining HP,
            <code>runCombat</code> reports a simple <code>boolean</code> victory flag so a larger
            campaign knows whether the hero won or fell.
        </p>
        <pre><code>public static boolean runCombat(String playerName, int playerHP, int playerAC,
                                int playerAttackBonus, String monsterName,
                                int monsterHP, int monsterAC,
                                int monsterAttackBonus, int monsterDamageDie)</code></pre>
        <h4>5e Round Rules &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>While both combatants have <code>HP &gt; 0</code>:
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Player strike:</strong> Roll <code>int d20 = Dice.roll(20);</code>.
                        Natural <code>20</code> always hits for <code>Dice.roll(8) * 2</code>;
                        Natural <code>1</code> always misses (<code>0</code> damage); otherwise if
                        <code>d20 + playerAttackBonus &gt;= monsterAC</code>, deal
                        <code>Dice.roll(8)</code> damage. Subtract from <code>monsterHP</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Monster counterattack (only if <code>monsterHP &gt; 0</code>):</strong>
                        Roll <code>int m20 = Dice.roll(20);</code>. If
                        <code>m20 + monsterAttackBonus &gt;= playerAC</code>, subtract
                        <code>Dice.roll(monsterDamageDie)</code> from <code>playerHP</code>.</li>
                </ol>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>boolean</code>):</strong> <code>true</code> if
                <code>playerHP &gt; 0</code>, or <code>false</code> otherwise.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>runCombat</code></h3>
        <p>Think through similarities to Part 1 and branch ordering:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Similarities &amp; Differences vs. Part 1:</strong> How does the outer
                <code>while</code> loop and monster-survival guard match
                <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code>, and what changes inside each round and in the final
                <code>return</code> (how can you write the final return in one line using Boolean
                Zen)?</li>
            <li><strong>Branch Priority Edge Case:</strong> Suppose a hero has
                <code>playerAttackBonus = +20</code> vs. a Goblin with <code>monsterAC = 8</code>,
                and rolls <code>d20 = 1</code>. Why must the <code>d20 == 20</code> and
                <code>d20 == 1</code> branches come <em>before</em>
                <code>d20 + playerAttackBonus &gt;= monsterAC</code> in your
                <code>if / else if</code> chain?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>runCombat</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and run <code>java DungeonLoop</code> to watch Aria
                battle the Cave Goblin before unlocking <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 1 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Parts 1 and 2 run cleanly in <code>java DungeonLoop</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code> below and pass all <strong>6 checks in Parts 1 and 2</strong> on your first run (Parts 3–4 are tomorrow!)</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/dungeon-system.html">Dungeon Loop System (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/while-loops.html">while Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
